Question: Which declaration correctly creates an immutable integer constant?
Options: {"options": ["const int MAX = 100;", "var MAX = 100;", "let MAX = 100;", "int MAX := 100;"]}
Explanation: const (or final) signals immutability; var/let without const are mutable.

Question: What is type inference?
Options: {"options": ["Compiler deduces type from initializer", "Type is checked only at runtime", "Variables have no type", "Types are guessed randomly"]}
Explanation: Inference is compile-time deduction from the initializer, not dynamic typing.

Question: Which type best represents a true/false decision?
Options: {"options": ["boolean", "int", "char", "float"]}
Explanation: Boolean has exactly two values and communicates intent without encoding tricks.

Question: Why prefer explicit types at public function boundaries?
Options: {"options": ["Documents the contract for callers", "It makes code run faster in all languages", "Implicit types are not allowed there", "It disables type checking"]}
Explanation: Explicit signatures are documentation and preserve API stability; inference is best locally.

Question: Which construct is clearest for many-way dispatch on shape?
Options: {"options": ["match/switch", "nested if/else", "goto", "while loop"]}
Explanation: match/switch expresses multi-way dispatch declaratively; long if/else chains obscure structure.

Question: When is a for loop preferred over while?
Options: {"options": ["When the iteration count is known", "When reading until a sentinel", "When recursion is required", "Never — while is always preferred"]}
Explanation: for loops encode known bounds (i < n) directly; while is for dynamic termination.

Question: What does a guard clause achieve?
Options: {"options": ["Returns early on invalid input to keep the happy path flat", "Wraps the whole function in try/catch", "Inverts the loop direction", "Marks code as deprecated"]}
Explanation: Guard clauses exit on edge cases first, preventing deep nesting of the main logic.

Question: Which loop guarantees at least one execution?
Options: {"options": ["do-while", "while", "for", "match"]}
Explanation: do-while checks the condition after the body, so it runs once even if the condition is initially false.

Question: What characterizes a pure function?
Options: {"options": ["Depends only on inputs, no side effects", "Modifies global state on each call", "Always runs in O(1) time", "Cannot be tested in isolation"]}
Explanation: Purity makes outputs deterministic from inputs and simplifies testing and reasoning.

Question: What does a closure capture?
Options: {"options": ["Variables from its defining environment", "Only its own parameters", "The entire heap", "A copy of all global variables"]}
Explanation: Closures retain lexical bindings even after the outer function has returned.

Question: When should parameters be bundled into a record/object?
Options: {"options": ["When a function takes more than three parameters", "Always — even for one parameter", "Only when recursion is used", "Only when the language forces it"]}
Explanation: Bundling clarifies call sites and groups coherent data; it also eases future evolution.

Question: What is the narrowest correct scope for a loop counter?
Options: {"options": ["Inside the loop header/block", "At the top of the file as global", "As a field of an unrelated class", "In the caller of the function"]}
Explanation: Scope should be minimal: declare the counter where it is used so it does not leak.

Question: Which topology isolates a cable failure to one host?
Options: {"options": ["Star", "Bus", "Ring", "Full mesh without switching"]}
Explanation: Star connects every host to a central switch; a single link failure affects only that host.

Question: What does full-duplex allow?
Options: {"options": ["Simultaneous two-way transmission", "One-way only", "Two-way, one direction at a time", "No transmission"]}
Explanation: Full-duplex carries traffic both ways at once; half-duplex alternates.

Question: Why layer protocols?
Options: {"options": ["Each layer handles one concern, relying on the layers below", "To make headers larger", "To avoid any specification", "Because one layer is always sufficient"]}
Explanation: Layering separates concerns; a layer uses the service of the layer below without knowing its internals.

Question: Which is a LAN example?
Options: {"options": ["Office floor network", "Transcontinental backbone", "The Internet", "Geostationary satellite network"]}
Explanation: LAN spans a single site; WAN spans cities, the Internet is global.

Question: How many layers does OSI have vs TCP/IP?
Options: {"options": ["7 vs 4", "4 vs 7", "5 vs 5", "7 vs 7"]}
Explanation: OSI has 7, TCP/IP collapses to 4 (Link, Internet, Transport, Application).

Question: What happens to headers on send?
Options: {"options": ["Each layer adds a header (encapsulation)", "Headers are removed", "Only the application adds a header", "Headers are encrypted and never added"]}
Explanation: Sending encapsulates: Application → Transport → Internet → Link, each adding a header.

Question: Which layer moves packets between hosts?
Options: {"options": ["Network (Internet) layer", "Physical layer only", "Session layer", "Presentation layer"]}
Explanation: Network/Internet layer handles host-to-host routing; Transport is end-to-end.

Question: When debugging, port closed indicates failure at which layer?
Options: {"options": ["Transport (L4)", "Physical (L1)", "Network (L3)", "Data Link (L2)"]}
Explanation: Port reachability is a Transport-layer concern (TCP/UDP).

Question: What does CIDR /24 mean?
Options: {"options": ["24 network bits, 8 host bits", "24 host bits, 8 network bits", "24 total bits", "24 addresses"]}
Explanation: /24 leaves 8 host bits → 254 usable hosts.

Question: How does a router choose a route?
Options: {"options": ["Longest-prefix match", "Shortest header", "First alphabetically", "Random"]}
Explanation: Routers pick the most specific (longest) matching prefix.

Question: What does NAT do?
Options: {"options": ["Shares one public IP among many private hosts", "Assigns every host a public IP", "Encrypts DNS queries", "Removes IP headers"]}
Explanation: NAT rewrites private addresses to a shared public address at the border.

Question: A mask 255.255.255.0 leaves how many host bits?
Options: {"options": ["8", "16", "24", "32"]}
Explanation: 255.255.255.0 = /24 → 8 host bits.

Question: What problem does a DBMS solve vs files?
Options: {"options": ["Centralizes data, enforces consistency, recovery, and concurrency", "Makes data permanently inconsistent", "Removes the need for any queries", "Stores data only in RAM"]}
Explanation: DBMS provides structure, guarantees, and recovery that per-application files cannot.

Question: Which describes the three-schema architecture?
Options: {"options": ["Physical, logical, view", "Network, transport, application", "Primary, foreign, candidate", "Select, insert, update"]}
Explanation: Physical is storage, logical is tables, view is per-application.

Question: What can a single DBMS log do after a crash?
Options: {"options": ["Restore the entire database", "Only restore one file", "Nothing", "Delete all data"]}
Explanation: A central log enables full recovery, unlike file-per-application.

Question: Which is NOT a DBMS advantage?
Options: {"options": ["Automatic enforcement of constraints and recovery", "Duplicated per-application recovery logic", "Centralized concurrency control", "Data abstraction"]}
Explanation: Duplicated logic is the file-system problem, not a DBMS advantage.

Question: What uniquely identifies a tuple?
Options: {"options": ["Primary key", "View", "Index", "Transaction"]}
Explanation: Primary key is the chosen minimal unique identifier.

Question: What does a foreign key point to?
Options: {"options": ["A primary key elsewhere", "A view definition", "A transaction log", "An index file"]}
Explanation: Foreign key references a primary key in another relation.

Question: Which integrity forbids null primary keys?
Options: {"options": ["Entity integrity", "Referential integrity", "Domain integrity", "ACID"]}
Explanation: Entity integrity requires every primary key to be non-null.

Question: Which forbids dangling foreign keys?
Options: {"options": ["Referential integrity", "Entity integrity", "Domain integrity", "Two-phase commit"]}
Explanation: Referential integrity guarantees every foreign key value exists as a primary key.

Question: Which SQL group creates tables?
Options: {"options": ["DDL", "DML", "DCL", "TCL"]}
Explanation: DDL (CREATE, ALTER, DROP) defines structure.

Question: What does a transaction guarantee?
Options: {"options": ["All statements commit or all roll back (atomicity)", "Only the first statement commits", "Statements are always concurrent", "No guarantees"]}
Explanation: Transactions are atomic units.

Question: Which isolation behaves as if transactions ran one by one?
Options: {"options": ["Serializable", "Read Uncommitted", "Read Committed", "Eventual"]}
Explanation: Serializable is the strongest, most blocking level.

Question: Dirty reads are prevented at which level and above?
Options: {"options": ["Read Committed and above", "Read Uncommitted only", "Serializable only", "Never"]}
Explanation: Read Committed sees only committed data; Read Uncommitted can see dirty reads.

Question: What does an OS multiplex?
Options: {"options": ["Hardware among programs", "Only the keyboard", "Only the network", "Nothing"]}
Explanation: OS shares CPU, memory, and I/O among programs with isolation.

Question: What is a system call?
Options: {"options": ["A gated trap from user to kernel mode", "A normal function call", "A hardware interrupt only", "A network packet"]}
Explanation: System calls are the narrow, validated gate to privileged operations.

Question: Why prevent user programs from touching hardware directly?
Options: {"options": ["To stop a buggy loop from overwriting the disk", "To make programs slower", "To save power only", "There is no reason"]}
Explanation: Isolation is the only thing preventing corruption.

Question: Which is a time-sharing OS example?
Options: {"options": ["Multics/Unix-like", "Single-purpose batch only", "No OS", "Firmware only"]}
Explanation: Time-sharing multiplexes interactive users.

Question: What does a PCB store?
Options: {"options": ["Process state, registers, scheduling info", "Only the program code", "Only the file name", "Nothing"]}
Explanation: PCB is the OS bookkeeping for a process.

Question: Why are thread switches cheaper than process switches?
Options: {"options": ["Threads share address space and files", "Threads use more memory", "Processes are always single-threaded", "There is no difference"]}
Explanation: Shared state avoids flushing address spaces.

Question: Which metric bounds interactive response?
Options: {"options": ["Response time (round-robin)", "Turnaround only", "Throughput only", "No metric"]}
Explanation: Round-robin bounds response time at cost of throughput.

Question: Which scheduling is convoy-prone but fair?
Options: {"options": ["FCFS", "SJF", "Round-robin", "Priority"]}
Explanation: FCFS (first-come first-served) suffers convoy effect.

Question: What gives each process a private address illusion?
Options: {"options": ["Virtual memory", "Physical memory only", "No memory", "Registers only"]}
Explanation: Virtual memory via page tables and MMU provides isolation.

Question: What caches page translations?
Options: {"options": ["TLB", "L1 cache only", "Disk", "NIC"]}
Explanation: TLB (translation lookaside buffer) caches page table entries.

Question: What happens on a stray pointer without virtual memory?
Options: {"options": ["Corrupts another process", "Always faults", "Is always safe", "Nothing"]}
Explanation: Without translation, a stray write corrupts; with it, it faults.

Question: Which replacement is considered steady state?
Options: {"options": ["LRU/Clock page replacement", "No replacement", "Single page", "No paging"]}
Explanation: Replacement is the steady state, not an edge case.

Question: What does O(n log n) typically describe?
Options: {"options": ["Sorting", "Constant access", "Halving search", "Quadratic scan"]}
Explanation: Efficient sorts like mergesort are O(n log n).

Question: Array access by index is?
Options: {"options": ["O(1)", "O(n)", "O(log n)", "O(n²)"]}
Explanation: Contiguous storage gives constant-time indexing.

Question: Insertion in the middle of an array is?
Options: {"options": ["O(n) due to shifting", "O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(0)"]}
Explanation: Elements after the insertion point must shift.

Question: Dynamic array amortized push is O(1) because?
Options: {"options": ["Capacity doubles when full", "It never copies", "It is always O(n)", "It uses a linked list"]}
Explanation: Doubling yields amortized O(1) despite occasional O(n) copy.

Question: Stack is?
Options: {"options": ["LIFO", "FIFO", "Random", "Sorted"]}
Explanation: Stack: last-in, first-out.

Question: Queue is?
Options: {"options": ["FIFO", "LIFO", "Random", "Sorted"]}
Explanation: Queue: first-in, first-out.

Question: Using a list with remove(0) as a queue is?
Options: {"options": ["O(n) per dequeue — poor", "O(1) — ideal", "O(log n)", "O(0)"]}
Explanation: Removing from index 0 shifts all elements.

Question: Stack is used for?
Options: {"options": ["Call frames, undo, bracket matching", "Scheduling BFS", "Sorting only", "Nothing"]}
Explanation: Stacks match LIFO use cases.

Question: BST search when balanced is?
Options: {"options": ["O(log n)", "O(n)", "O(1)", "O(n²)"]}
Explanation: Balanced BST search halves each step.

Question: Inorder traversal of BST yields?
Options: {"options": ["Sorted order", "Random order", "Reverse order", "No order"]}
Explanation: Inorder visits left, node, right → sorted.

Question: Adjacency list space is?
Options: {"options": ["O(V+E)", "O(V²)", "O(1)", "O(E²)"]}
Explanation: List stores vertices plus edges.

Question: BFS uses?
Options: {"options": ["Queue", "Stack", "Array only", "Heap"]}
Explanation: BFS level-by-level via queue; DFS via stack.

Question: Which component executes Java bytecode?
Options: {"options": ["Java Virtual Machine (JVM)", "Java compiler (javac)", "Java documentation tool", "Java archiver"]}
Explanation: The JVM executes bytecode on each platform; javac only compiles source to bytecode.

Question: Which method is the entry point of a Java application?
Options: {"options": ["public void start()", "public static void main(String[] args)", "static int main()", "void init()"]}
Explanation: The JVM looks for the exact main signature to start the program.

Question: What does the JDK include that the JRE alone does not?
Options: {"options": ["The virtual machine", "The class libraries", "Development tools such as the compiler", "The garbage collector"]}
Explanation: The JDK bundles the JRE plus development tools like javac.

Question: What is an object in Java?
Options: {"options": ["An instance of a class holding state and behavior", "A primitive data type", "A package of classes", "A compiled bytecode file"]}
Explanation: Objects are class instances with fields (state) and methods (behavior).

Question: Which keyword creates a new object?
Options: {"options": ["create", "alloc", "new", "instance"]}
Explanation: The new keyword allocates the object and invokes its constructor.

Question: Where are Java objects stored in memory?
Options: {"options": ["On the stack", "In registers", "In source files", "On the heap"]}
Explanation: Objects live on the heap and are reclaimed by the garbage collector.

Question: Which keyword establishes inheritance between classes?
Options: {"options": ["extends", "inherits", "super", "implements"]}
Explanation: A class extends its parent; implements is reserved for interfaces.

Question: What does a super() call invoke?
Options: {"options": ["The current method", "A static method", "The parent class constructor", "The subclass constructor"]}
Explanation: super() must be the first statement and chains to the parent constructor.

Question: Which statement about method overriding is true?
Options: {"options": ["The method must be static", "The method must be private", "Overriding requires a different method name", "The subclass provides its own implementation of an inherited method"]}
Explanation: Overriding keeps the signature and replaces the inherited behavior.

Question: Which block always executes after try and catch?
Options: {"options": ["finally", "final", "finalize", "catch-all"]}
Explanation: finally runs for cleanup whether or not an exception occurred.

Question: Which keyword declares that a method may throw an exception?
Options: {"options": ["throw", "try", "throws", "catch"]}
Explanation: throws is part of the method signature; throw raises a specific instance.

Question: What is the root of the Java exception hierarchy?
Options: {"options": ["Exception", "Error", "RuntimeException", "Throwable"]}
Explanation: Throwable is the root; Exception and Error both extend it.

Question: Which process model proceeds through phases without going back?
Options: {"options": ["Waterfall", "Spiral", "Agile Scrum", "Iterative"]}
Explanation: Waterfall flows linearly through requirements to maintenance with no planned return.

Question: What does SDLC stand for?
Options: {"options": ["System Design and Logic Code", "Software Development Life Cycle", "Software Deployment and Launch Control", "Structured Data Lifecycle"]}
Explanation: SDLC names the end-to-end phases software passes through.

Question: Which approach delivers working software in short sprints?
Options: {"options": ["Waterfall", "V-model", "Agile Scrum", "Big bang"]}
Explanation: Scrum organizes work into short inspect-and-adapt sprints.

Question: What is the main goal of object-oriented analysis?
Options: {"options": ["To model the problem domain with objects and responsibilities", "To write code as fast as possible", "To design the database schema first", "To draw deployment diagrams"]}
Explanation: Analysis builds an object model of the problem before design decisions.

Question: Which activity comes first in OOAD?
Options: {"options": ["Implementation", "Analysis of requirements", "Testing", "Deployment"]}
Explanation: OOAD starts by analyzing requirements into candidate objects.

Question: What is a responsibility in OOAD?
Options: {"options": ["A Java package", "A test case", "A duty assigned to an object: knowing or doing", "A sprint backlog item"]}
Explanation: Responsibility-driven design assigns knowing and doing duties to objects.

Question: What does a class diagram show?
Options: {"options": ["Classes with attributes, operations and relationships", "Object memory addresses", "Runtime message order", "Deployment servers"]}
Explanation: Class diagrams capture static structure: classes plus their links.

Question: What does the multiplicity 1..* mean?
Options: {"options": ["Zero or one instance", "Exactly one instance", "One or more instances", "Many to many always"]}
Explanation: The lower bound 1 requires at least one linked instance.

Question: Which UML relationship expresses the strongest whole-part ownership?
Options: {"options": ["Association", "Dependency", "Generalization", "Composition"]}
Explanation: Composition owns its parts: destroying the whole destroys the parts.

Question: What is an actor in a use case diagram?
Options: {"options": ["An external role interacting with the system", "A Java class", "A test runner", "A database table"]}
Explanation: Actors are external users or systems, not internal classes.

Question: What does an include relationship mean?
Options: {"options": ["Optional behavior at runtime", "A use case always incorporates another use case", "An actor logs in", "A diagram border style"]}
Explanation: Include factors out mandatory shared behavior into a reused use case.

Question: What bounds the scope of the modelled system?
Options: {"options": ["The actor icon", "The extend arrow", "The system boundary rectangle", "The package tab"]}
Explanation: Everything inside the boundary is the system; actors stay outside.

Question: What is a conceptual class in a domain model?
Options: {"options": ["A Java source file", "A database table", "A domain idea, not a software class", "A unit test"]}
Explanation: Domain models capture real-world concepts before software design.

Question: Domain models are mainly derived from what?
Options: {"options": ["Code comments", "Sprint velocity", "Server logs", "Use cases and requirements"]}
Explanation: Noun phrases in use cases reveal candidate conceptual classes.

Question: Which statement belongs in a domain model?
Options: {"options": ["public static void main", "SELECT * FROM users", "Sprint retrospective notes", "Customer places Order"]}
Explanation: Domain models record business facts, never code or SQL.

Question: What does HTML stand for?
Options: {"options": ["HyperText Markup Language", "HighText Machine Language", "Hyperlink Text Model", "Home Tool Markup Language"]}
Explanation: HTML is the markup language that structures web pages.

Question: Which tag defines the largest heading?
Options: {"options": ["<h6>", "<head>", "<h1>", "<header>"]}
Explanation: Heading levels run h1 (largest) to h6 (smallest).

Question: Which element is semantic HTML5?
Options: {"options": ["<div>", "<span>", "<b>", "<article>"]}
Explanation: Semantic elements like article describe meaning; div and span carry none.

Question: Which keyword declares a block-scoped variable in JavaScript?
Options: {"options": ["let", "int", "float", "string"]}
Explanation: let (and const) are block-scoped; int and float are not JS keywords.

Question: What is the result of typeof [] in JavaScript?
Options: {"options": ["array", "list", "object", "undefined"]}
Explanation: Arrays are objects in JS; Array.isArray performs the real check.

Question: What does === compare in JavaScript?
Options: {"options": ["Value with type conversion", "Only object references", "Only string length", "Value and type without conversion"]}
Explanation: Strict equality skips coercion, unlike ==.

Question: Which servlet method handles HTTP GET requests?
Options: {"options": ["doGet", "doPost", "serviceGet", "handleGet"]}
Explanation: The container dispatches GET to doGet and POST to doPost.

Question: What is the servlet lifecycle order?
Options: {"options": ["service, init, destroy", "destroy, init, service", "init, service, destroy", "init, destroy, service"]}
Explanation: The container initializes once, services each request, then destroys.

Question: Where do Java servlets execute?
Options: {"options": ["In the browser", "In the database", "On the GPU", "In a servlet container such as Tomcat"]}
Explanation: Servlets run server-side inside a container managing their lifecycle.

Question: Which character starts a PHP variable name?
Options: {"options": ["$", "#", "@", "%"]}
Explanation: Every PHP variable begins with a dollar sign.

Question: Which superglobal holds submitted form POST data?
Options: {"options": ["$POST", "$_FORM", "$_POST", "$postData"]}
Explanation: PHP populates $_POST with posted form fields.

Question: Where do PHP scripts execute?
Options: {"options": ["In the browser DOM", "In the CSS engine", "On the server before the page is sent", "On the network router"]}
Explanation: PHP is server-side: it renders output delivered to the browser.

Question: What is JSX?
Options: {"options": ["A syntax extension mixing HTML-like markup with JavaScript", "A database query language", "A CSS preprocessor", "A test framework"]}
Explanation: JSX compiles to React.createElement calls describing UI.

Question: What is the virtual DOM in React?
Options: {"options": ["The browser window object", "A shadow router", "An in-memory representation React diffs to update efficiently", "A JSX compiler pass"]}
Explanation: Diffing minimizes real DOM writes to what actually changed.

Question: Which code defines a React function component?
Options: {"options": ["class Welcome extends Servlet", "SELECT COMPONENT Welcome", "<component>Welcome</component>", "function Welcome(props) returning JSX"]}
Explanation: Function components are plain functions of props returning JSX.

Question: What is an intelligent agent?
Options: {"options": ["An entity perceiving its environment and acting on it", "A sorting algorithm", "A database index", "A CSS rule"]}
Explanation: Agents close the loop from sensors through decisions to actuators.

Question: What does PEAS specify for an agent design?
Options: {"options": ["Program, Export, Analyze, Save", "Performance, Environment, Actuators, Sensors", "Perceptron, Error, Activation, Synapse", "Plan, Execute, Assert, Stop"]}
Explanation: PEAS frames what the agent optimizes, senses and affects.

Question: Which agent acts on condition-action rules from the current percept?
Options: {"options": ["Utility agent", "Learning agent", "Goal agent", "Simple reflex agent"]}
Explanation: Simple reflex agents map the current percept directly to an action.

Question: What does Bayes rule compute?
Options: {"options": ["Posterior probability from prior and likelihood", "The shortest path in a graph", "The maximum flow value", "The gradient magnitude"]}
Explanation: Bayes rule updates beliefs by combining prior with observed evidence.

Question: What is a prior in Bayesian reasoning?
Options: {"options": ["The final answer", "A search heuristic", "Belief before seeing evidence", "A loss value"]}
Explanation: The prior seeds inference and the posterior refines it with data.

Question: The posterior is proportional to what?
Options: {"options": ["Prior minus likelihood", "Likelihood divided by error", "Evidence alone", "Likelihood times prior"]}
Explanation: Posterior scales with likelihood times prior, normalized by evidence.

Question: What does linear regression predict?
Options: {"options": ["A continuous numeric value", "A class label only", "A cluster identifier", "A parse tree"]}
Explanation: Regression maps inputs to continuous targets like price or temperature.

Question: Which cost function is minimized in basic linear regression?
Options: {"options": ["Cross entropy", "Mean squared error", "Hinge loss", "Zero-one loss"]}
Explanation: MSE penalizes squared residuals and yields the least-squares fit.

Question: What is overfitting?
Options: {"options": ["Under-training a model", "Missing input values", "Fitting noise so new data suffers", "Slow convergence"]}
Explanation: Overfit models memorize training noise and generalize poorly.

Question: Why do ensembles of diverse models help?
Options: {"options": ["They train faster in all cases", "They need less data in all cases", "Combining diverse models reduces error", "They remove feature engineering"]}
Explanation: Averaging uncorrelated errors beats most single models.

Question: What is voting in ensemble methods?
Options: {"options": ["Deleting weak models", "Splitting the dataset", "Aggregating member predictions", "Tuning the learning rate"]}
Explanation: Voting or averaging fuses member outputs into one decision.

Question: Bagging-style ensembles mainly reduce which error component?
Options: {"options": ["Bias in all cases", "Dataset size", "Feature count", "Variance"]}
Explanation: Averaging independent-ish models damps variance while keeping bias.

Question: What is a perceptron?
Options: {"options": ["A linear threshold unit combining weighted inputs", "A clustering method", "A sorting network", "A regular expression engine"]}
Explanation: The perceptron fires when the weighted sum crosses a threshold.

Question: Which functions can a single perceptron learn?
Options: {"options": ["Any function", "Only constant functions", "Linearly separable functions", "XOR of its inputs"]}
Explanation: One linear boundary cannot separate XOR-like patterns.

Question: What does the perceptron learning rule adjust?
Options: {"options": ["The dataset size", "The activation name", "Weights after misclassification", "The random seed"]}
Explanation: Weights move to correct mistakes and freeze once separated.

Question: What is CRISP-DM?
Options: {"options": ["A data mining process methodology", "A chart type", "A Python library", "A file format"]}
Explanation: CRISP-DM structures projects from business understanding to deployment.

Question: Which of these is a data science role?
Options: {"options": ["Network router", "CSS designer", "Data analyst", "DNS administrator"]}
Explanation: Analysts turn data into decisions; the rest are unrelated roles.

Question: What comes first in a data science project?
Options: {"options": ["Training models", "Deploying dashboards", "Understanding the business problem", "Tuning GPUs"]}
Explanation: Clear goals precede data collection and modelling.

Question: What is exploratory data analysis?
Options: {"options": ["Exploring data with summaries and plots before modelling", "Exporting data archives", "Encrypting datasets", "Erasing duplicates only"]}
Explanation: EDA builds intuition about distributions and quirks first.

Question: Which plot best reveals a distribution shape?
Options: {"options": ["Pie chart of everything", "Histogram", "Scatter of row ids", "Bar of names only"]}
Explanation: Histograms bin values to expose shape, skew and modes.

Question: What is an outlier?
Options: {"options": ["A missing value", "A column name", "A value far from the rest of the data", "A file header"]}
Explanation: Outliers may signal errors or genuinely rare events to handle.

Question: What is a NumPy ndarray?
Options: {"options": ["A fast N-dimensional array container", "A text file format", "A plot window", "A SQL table"]}
Explanation: ndarrays store typed data contiguously for vectorized speed.

Question: What is NumPy broadcasting?
Options: {"options": ["Sending network packets", "Plotting bar charts", "Applying operations across compatible shapes", "Joining tables"]}
Explanation: Broadcasting stretches smaller shapes without copying data.

Question: Which operation is vectorized?
Options: {"options": ["A Python for loop appending items", "Manual per-index arithmetic", "arr * 2 on a NumPy array", "String concatenation in a loop"]}
Explanation: Vectorized ops run in compiled code instead of Python loops.

Question: What is the Figure in Matplotlib?
Options: {"options": ["The top-level container of a plot", "A legend entry", "A color map", "An axis tick"]}
Explanation: Figures hold Axes, which hold the actual plotted artists.

Question: Which call draws a line plot?
Options: {"options": ["plt.table", "plt.text", "plt.plot", "plt.savefig"]}
Explanation: plt.plot renders line and marker series on the axes.

Question: What does a plot legend communicate?
Options: {"options": ["The file size on disk", "Labels mapping plotted artists to meaning", "The axis limits", "The import list"]}
Explanation: Legends decode which line or color stands for what.

Question: What does worst-case analysis measure?
Options: {"options": ["The maximum cost over all inputs of a given size", "The average luck across runs", "The best possible hope", "The length of the source code"]}
Explanation: Worst case bounds the cost no matter how adversarial the input.

Question: Which dimensions measure algorithm efficiency?
Options: {"options": ["Lines of comments", "Time and space growth", "Variable name length", "Number of source files"]}
Explanation: Efficiency is asymptotic time versus input size plus memory used.

Question: What is an algorithm?
Options: {"options": ["Any program bug", "A hardware chip", "A data file", "A finite step-by-step procedure solving a problem"]}
